<div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><section class="snap"><h2>Pikeville at a glance</h2>
<dl class="snap-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Pike County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Eastern Kentucky</dd></div><div><dt>Housing mix</dt><dd>Homes cluster along creeks and river valleys, with older frame houses, mobile and manufactured homes, brick ranches and newer construction on graded hillside lots.</dd></div></dl>
<h3>Local claim risks</h3><ul class="tags"><li>Flash flooding</li><li>Hillside slides</li><li>Mine subsidence</li><li>Windstorm and falling trees</li><li>Frozen pipes</li></ul>
<h3>Neighborhoods and nearby communities</h3><ul class="tags light"><li>Downtown Pikeville</li><li>Coal Run Village</li><li>Shelbiana</li><li>Elkhorn City</li><li>Belfry</li><li>Phelps</li><li>Virgie</li><li>Robinson Creek</li></ul>
<h3>Weather history</h3><ol class="mini-tl"><li><b>1977</b><span>April floods on the Levisa and Tug forks inundated Pikeville and spurred the Pikeville Cut-Through project.</span></li><li><b>2022</b><span>The July floods devastated eastern Kentucky, with catastrophic damage in counties across the region.</span></li><li><b>2025</b><span>February flooding swept through Pikeville and Pike County, damaging homes and businesses along waterways.</span></li></ol></section>
<h2>Valleys, Creeks and Fast Water</h2>
<p>In Pike County, houses are built where the land is flat enough, and that usually means near a creek or river. Heavy rain in steep terrain moves fast, and flash flooding can reach homes that never flooded before. The 1977 flood led to the massive Pikeville Cut-Through, and recent floods have shown how quickly water still rises in the hollows.</p>
<p>The essential coverage point: <strong>flood is excluded</strong> from standard homeowners policies and requires separate flood insurance. But not every water claim is flood. Wind-driven rain through a damaged roof, water from a broken pipe and certain backups are treated differently, and those amounts can go to appraisal.</p>
<h2>Mine Subsidence and Hillside Movement</h2>
<p>Coal mining has shaped Pike County for generations, and underground workings can cause the ground above to sink. Kentucky operates a Mine Subsidence Insurance Fund, and in designated coal counties that coverage is generally offered alongside homeowners policies, though owners should confirm whether they have it and what it pays. Standard policies otherwise <strong>exclude earth movement</strong>, including landslides on saturated hillsides.</p>
<p>When damage appears, the question is often what caused it: mine subsidence, natural slope movement, a broken water line or poor drainage. The answer decides which coverage, if any, applies. We document the pattern of cracking and settlement carefully so the cause question can be addressed before the cost question.</p>
<h2>Pricing Repairs in the Mountains</h2>
<p>Construction in eastern Kentucky has real cost factors that generic estimates miss:</p>
<ul>
<li>Travel time and limited contractor availability after regional disasters.</li>
<li>Difficult access for equipment on narrow roads and steep lots.</li>
<li>Steep roofs and multi-level foundations requiring extra labor.</li>
<li>Manufactured homes with components that must be sourced specifically.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Document early</b>After a flood, photograph water lines on walls and contents before cleanup. Those photos are hard to replace later.</div>
<h2>Serving Pike County and the Coalfields</h2>
<p>We come to the property, whether it sits in Pikeville or up a hollow near Elkhorn City or Belfry. Our estimates account for local conditions and contractor realities. If you are unsure where the process starts, our <a href="/kentucky-appraisal-process/">Kentucky appraisal process</a> overview walks through each step, and the <a href="/insurance-appraisal/water-damage-appraisal/">water damage appraisal</a> page explains how we sort covered water from excluded flood.</p>
<p>Eastern Kentucky owners have been through more than their share of disasters, and many have learned the hard way that a quick first payment is not always the full value of the claim. Taking time to understand what the policy covers, and to document what the repair truly requires, usually leads to a fairer result for the household.</p>
<h2>Appraisal services available in Pikeville</h2><p>Every Kentucky Insurance Appraiser service is available to owners in Pikeville and across Pike County, including:</p><ul><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/party-appraiser-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/home-insurance-appraisal/">Homeowners Insurance Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/roof-hail-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/tornado-wind-appraisal/">Tornado &amp; Wind Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/fire-smoke-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Pikeville claim questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is mine subsidence covered in Pike County?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Kentucky’s Mine Subsidence Insurance Fund provides coverage in designated coal counties, generally offered with homeowners policies. Check your declarations to confirm you have it.</p></details><details><summary>Can appraisal help after a flash flood?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Only for water damage from a covered cause. Flood itself is excluded from homeowners policies and handled under separate flood insurance.</p></details><details><summary>Will you travel to remote parts of Pike County?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Yes. We inspect on site throughout Pike County and the rest of eastern Kentucky.</p></details></div>
